  "questionId": "q14",
  "speechText": "For me, the first step is to give the model the right and limited context. The main reason is that if the context is wrong, even a strong model can give a very confident wrong answer. For example, for knowledge-based questions, I use good retrieval and relevant evidence instead of depending only on the model's own knowledge. After that, I use structured output and backend validation when the result is used by the system. If the model suggests an action, the backend still checks the business rules and permissions. I also adjust the model settings based on the task. For factual tasks, I usually prefer a lower temperature to make the output more consistent and less random. At the same time, I use eval cases for important scenarios. If the model is not sure, I prefer to ask for clarification, require confirmation, or use a safe fallback instead of letting it guess. So overall, hallucination cannot be completely removed, but we can reduce the risk with better context, retrieval, validation, proper model settings, evals, and safe fallback.",
